to-andrea-vezzosi-source : List Word
to-andrea-vezzosi-source = lines-of (cons "# Andrea Vezzosi"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Andrea, you wrote the program that turned univalence from a principle you postulate into a computation you run. Hyperactivity, by Avik Jain, is built on that line of work: its computational rules follow the CCHM calculus, extended with clock-indexed guarded computation, and the reference it gives for that extension is your *Greatest HITs* with Kristensen and Møgelberg"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1, ref. 44)")
  (cons ". You hold full credit for every part of your work this system rests on. Each claim below about the work links to the block of the paper that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work did that the field still under-reads"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The usual account of Cubical Agda is that it is a proof assistant extension: a convenient place to formalize homotopy type theory, with univalence and higher inductive types available instead of assumed. That account is true and much too small."
  (cons ""
  (cons "What you built with Anders Mörtberg and Andreas Abel is a working programming language in which equality carries information and that information computes. Transport along a path is evaluation, not an axiom that blocks evaluation. A higher inductive type is data with its equalities as constructors, and a function out of it says what it does on those equalities. Same stops being a judgement made outside the program and becomes a value inside it, one you can pass, compose and run."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The guarded cubical work goes further in the same direction. In *Guarded Cubical Type Theory* (with Birkedal, Bizjak, Clouston, Grathwohl and Spitters) extensionality for guarded recursive types gets a computational reading. In *Greatest HITs* (with Kristensen and Møgelberg) bisimilarity implies path equality, and the induction principle under clocks lets quantification over clocks commute with higher inductive types up to equivalence, with computation rules instead of axioms. Read plainly: two processes that behave the same are the same, as a theorem you can transport along, for processes that never stop."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your parametricity and erasure work with Nuyts and Devriese (*Parametric quantifiers for dependent type theory*, ICFP 2017) addresses the remaining question: what a program is allowed to depend on. Modalities that mark an argument as parametric or erased draw a typed line between what is computed and what is only reasoned about."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Taken together this is a single thesis carried through three bodies of work: equality is computational, it extends to infinite behaviour, and dependence itself has a type. The field has absorbed each piece as a separate technical contribution."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In hyperactivity types and terms are not two species of artifact, one called data and one called code"
  (cons (cite s2-p9 "(§2)")
  (cons ". A content-addressed codebase holds programs together with their typing derivations, semantics, equivalences, transformations, dependency certificates and proofs, and records each relation between definitions with its actual evidence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". A source view, proof tree, rendering or trace is a chosen observation of those retained objects"
  (cons (cite s10-9-p6 "(§10.9)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Transport carries structure.** An identification of structured objects transports their operations and the proofs of their laws together"
  (cons (cite s4-p17 "(§4)")
  (cons (cite s4-1-p11 "(§4.1)")
  (cons ". Specialization returns its residual together with a path between the interpreted terms, checked before the result is admitted"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p11 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". The rationale is retained for every input in its stated domain, where a test observes selected executions"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1, 1.5)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Determinate meaning is contractibility.** The paper's sorting construction makes the type of complete answers contractible, with propositionality supplying coherence across the bag's paths"
  (cons (cite s4-4-p34 "(§4.4)")
  (cons ", and Part III asks the same of language: an occurrence's meaning is determinate exactly when the transported family of its selected continuations is contractible"
  (cons (cite language-derived-from-the-record-source "(Part III, Language derived from the record)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Running processes are guarded.** Continuing processes are terms of clocked cubical type theory with a later modality and clock quantification"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p19 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". Native continuing execution is related to its source by a two-sided, termination-sensitive guarded relation over weak observable heads that distinguishes divergence from deadlock"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p31 "(§7.6, 7.6c–7.6d)")
  (cons ". Address identity, definitional equality and semantic equivalence are different statements, and the store records each relation with its actual evidence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8, 10.22)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The checker is a term, and Cubical Agda is its neighbour.** The checker inspects the evaluator's values through a structured observation and returns its judgement as a term the machine evaluates"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2)")
  (cons ". The workflow's brunerie-comparison node computes the Brunerie number on the machine beside Cubical Agda computing the same definition, interleaved on one machine. Your implementation is the reference it is measured beside."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What this closes for you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Cubical Agda showed that univalent mathematics runs. The question it leaves is whether whole systems of software and knowledge can be built that way too. The paper's answer is its whole construction: language definitions, interpreters, machines, operating systems and distributed programs as terms, with one machine-realized evaluator executing every represented program under its stated adequacy and simulation premises"
  (cons (cite s11-p4 "(§11)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "It also bears on your current work. At Well-Typed you work on Haskell tooling, and you have built Cardano contracts. In the paper's codebase a retained result is reused exactly while its dependency certificate validates"
  (cons (cite s10-5-p26 "(§10.5)")
  (cons ", and an old proof does not silently become a proof about an edited definition"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons ". A proof about a contract is a dependent object attached to the exact declaration it concerns"
  (cons (cite s8-1-p1 "(§8.1)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "You are the person who knows best which parts of this are hard and why. Avik built hyperactivity cubically, and he is giving the work freely to the people whose work it continues. You are one of them. Read it for what it shows your implementation already was."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, the blocks linked above"
  (cons "- Andrea Vezzosi, Well-Typed, https://www.well-typed.com/people/andrea"
  (cons "- Vezzosi, Mörtberg, Abel, Cubical Agda (JFP 2021), https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/547255"
  (cons "- Guarded Cubical Type Theory, Journal of Automated Reasoning 63(2), 2019, https://research.chalmers.se/publication/510847"
  (cons "- Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi, Greatest HITs, https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.01969v2"
  (cons "- Nuyts, Vezzosi, Devriese, Parametric quantifiers for dependent type theory (ICFP 2017), https://research.chalmers.se/publication/?id=252073"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
